     sswwaappoonn, sswwaappooffff, sswwaappccttll — specify devices for paging and swapping

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     sswwaappoonn [--FF _f_s_t_a_b] --aaLLqq | --EE _f_i_l_e _._._.
     sswwaappooffff [--FF _f_s_t_a_b] --aaffLLqq | _f_i_l_e _._._.
     sswwaappccttll [--AAffgghhkkllmmssUU] [--aa _f_i_l_e _._._. | --dd _f_i_l_e _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The sswwaappoonn, sswwaappooffff and sswwaappccttll utilities are used to control swap de‐
     vices in the system.  At boot time all swap entries in _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b are
     added automatically when the system goes multi-user.  Swap devices use a
     fixed interleave; the maximum number of devices is unlimited.  There is
     no priority mechanism.

     The sswwaappoonn utility adds the specified swap devices to the system.  If the
     --aa option is used, all swap devices in _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b will be added, unless
     their “noauto” or “late” option is also set.  If the --LL option is speci‐
     fied, swap devices with the “late” option will be added as well as ones
     with no option.  If the --qq option is used, informational messages will
     not be written to standard output when a swap device is added.  The --EE
     option causes each of following devices to receive a BIO_DELETE command.
     This command marks the device's blocks as unused, except those that might
     store a disk label.  This marking can erase a crash dump.  To delay
     sswwaappoonn for a device until after ssaavveeccoorree has copied the crash dump to an‐
     other location, use the “late” option.

     The sswwaappooffff utility removes the specified swap devices from the system.
     If the --aa option is used, all swap devices in _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b will be removed,
     unless their “noauto” option is also set.  If the --LL option is specified,
     only swap devices with the “late” option will be removed.  If the --qq op‐
     tion is used, informational messages will not be written to standard out‐
     put when a swap device is removed.  Note that sswwaappooffff will fail and
     refuse to remove a swap device if a very conservative check does not con‐
     clude that there is sufficient VM (memory + remaining swap devices) to
     run the system.  The --ff option turns off this check, which could deadlock
     the system if there is insufficient swap space remaining.

     The sswwaappooffff utility must move swapped pages out of the device being re‐
     moved which could lead to high system loads for a period of time, depend‐
     ing on how much data has been swapped out to that device.

     Other options supported by both sswwaappoonn and sswwaappooffff are as follows:

     --FF _f_s_t_a_b
             Specify the _f_s_t_a_b file to use (in conjunction with --aa).

     The sswwaappccttll utility exists primarily for those familiar with other BSDs
     and may be used to add, remove, or list swap devices.  Note that the --aa
     option is used differently in sswwaappccttll and indicates that a specific list
     of devices should be added.  The --dd option indicates that a specific list
     should be removed.  The --AA and --UU options to sswwaappccttll operate on all swap
     entries in _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b which do not have their “noauto” option set.

     Swap information can be generated using the swapinfo(8) utility, ppssttaatt
     --ss, or sswwaappccttll --ll.  The sswwaappccttll utility has the following options for
     listing swap:

     --hh      Output values in human-readable form.

     --gg      Output values in gigabytes.

     --kk      Output values in kilobytes.

     --mm      Output values in megabytes.

     --ll      List the devices making up system swap.

     --ss      Print a summary line for system swap.

             The BLOCKSIZE environment variable is used if not specifically
             overridden.  512 byte blocks are used by default.

